id":"la-villette-greenhouse"},{"time":527.92,"title":"Bernard Tschumi","id":"bernard-tschumi"},{"time":552.6,"title":"Frank Newby","id":"frank-newby"},{"time":632.24,"title":"InterAction Centre","id":"interaction-centre"},{"time":646.56,"title":"Trade Union Congress","id":"trade-union-congress"},{"time":662.72,"title":"London Zoo Aviary","id":"london-zoo-aviary"},{"time":676.6,"title":"FUN PALACE","id":"fun-palace"}]},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","jobtitle":"Architectural writer and editor","recorded":"2014-10-30","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/8f6f7744b941fb8eae6c60abd85ab5da/manifest/video.m3u8","start":0,"end":70,"topicId":"building","segments":[{"start":0,"end":70,"topicId":"building"}],"cards":[]},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","jobtitle":"Former member of the office, architect","recorded":"2014-11-05","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/005dfe1884685dc09eac01133744f2ef/manifest/video.m3u8","start":392,"end":496,"topicId":"building","segments":[{"start":392,"end":496,"topicId":"building"}],"cards":[{"time":421.56,"title":"La Villette Greenhouse","id":"la-villette-greenhouse"}]}],"cards":{"AA":{"slug":"aa","body":"The Architectural Association School of Architecture in London, commonly referred to as the AA, is the oldest independent school of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture\">architecture</a> in the UK and one of the most prestigious and competitive in the world. Its wide-ranging programme of exhibitions, lectures, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Academic_conference\">symposia</a> and publications have given it a central position in global discussions and developments within contemporary architectural culture.","subtitle":"Architectural Association School of Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/aa.jpg","external":"http://www.aaschool.ac.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/36+Bedford+Square,+AA+School+of+Architecture,+Fitzrovia,+London+WC1B+3ES,+UK/@51.5183066,-0.1307022,17z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":702,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":1172.2,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":195.92,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":388.16,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":483.92,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":636.36,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":3.32,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":527.72,"topicId":"scene","topicLabel":"Scene"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":657.92,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":764.56,"topicId":"technology","topicLabel":"Technology"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":453.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":641.96,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1068.04,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1965.44,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":295.64,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":636.52,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":884.76,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":266.96,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":350.88,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":575,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":738.8,"topicId":"appearance","topicLabel":"Appearance"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":498.32,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":518.88,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"BAT HAT":{"slug":"bat-hat","body":"With Battersea Power Station’s future once again in doubt, its new owner might wish it could add Cedric Price’s Bathat scheme to the list of options for the site.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\nPrice argued that trying to preserve it was the wrong approach. In 1984, when its owner held a limited architectural competition for the site, he launched his counterproposal in BD. This involved removing the lateral brick walls — the source of most maintenance problems — while supporting the upper levels and four chimneys on the existing steel structure. He pointed out that a signifi-cant part of the building had little historical value and was not worth the huge conser-vation costs, but by keeping the control room and chimneys as a celebrated symbol of London’s skyline, the land could be be released for redevelopment. He was deliberately vague on what could go on the site, believing an architect’s role was only to widen options not narrow them.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;","subtitle":"Cedric Price","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/bat-hat.jpg","external":"http://www.bdonline.co.uk/battersea-power-play/3088227.article#","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Battersea+Power+Station/@51.4818057,-0.1470724,16z","also":[{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":216.96,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":236.88,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":242.6,"topicId":"bathat","topicLabel":"BATHAT"}]},"Bernard Tschumi":{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","body":"Bernard Tschumi is widely recognized as one of today’s foremost architects. First known as a theorist, he drew attention to his innovative architectural practice in 1983 when he won the prestigious competition for the Parc de La Villette, a 125-acre cultural park based on activities as much as nature. The intertwining <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tschumi.com/projects/hyperword/Concept/\">concept</a>s of “<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.tschumi.com/projects/hyperword/Event/\">event</a>” and “movement” in architecture are supported by Tschumi’s belief that architecture is the most important innovation of our time. Tschumi often references other disciplines in his work, such as literature and film, proving that architecture must participate in culture’s polemics and question its foundations.","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/bernard-tschumi.jpg","external":"http://www.tschumi.com/bernard-tschumi/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Bernard+Tschumi+Architects/@40.741122,-73.999563,17z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":608.72,"topicId":"vilette","topicLabel":"VILETTE"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":370.8,"topicId":"englishness","topicLabel":"Englishness"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":211.76,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":527.92,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"Claude Nicolas Ledoux":{"slug":"claude-nicolas-ledoux","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Claude-Nicolas Ledoux (21 March 1736 – 18 November 1806) was one of the earliest exponents of French <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoclassical_architecture\">Neoclassical architecture</a>. He used his knowledge of architectural theory to design not only in domestic architecture but town planning; as a consequence of his visionary plan for the Ideal City of Chaux, he became known as a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utopia\">utopian</a>.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":317.08,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"Claude Parent & Paul Virilio":{"slug":"claude-parent-paul-virilio-2","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Claude Parent, born on 23 February 1923 at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuilly-sur-Seine\">Neuilly-sur-Seine</a>, France, is a French <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> known for his buildings featuring sloped floors, an aesthetic initially inspired by Parent's discovery, with philosopher <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Virilio\">Paul Virilio</a>, of World War II bunkers constructed along the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Wall\">Atlantic Wall</a> that had accidentally slipped down sand dunes because of severe winter weather. \"Inside [the fallen bunkers], you tumbled through a strange room; the floor was so sloped that you couldn't tell whether what you were standing on was a slanted floor or a wall,\" Parent has said. With Virilio, who was also a teacher at the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89cole_sp%C3%A9ciale_d%27architecture\">École spéciale d’architecture</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris\">Paris</a>, they educated many contemporary architects, such as Jean Nouvel. He won the Grand Prix national de l'architecture in 1979 and was elected at the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acad%C3%A9mie_des_Beaux-Arts\">Académie des Beaux-Arts</a> in 2005.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":341.04,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1224.48,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"}]},"Étienne-Louis Boullée":{"slug":"etienne-louis-boullee","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Étienne-Louis Boullée (February 12, 1728 – February 4, 1799) was a visionary <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France\">French</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoclassicism\">neoclassical</a> <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> whose work greatly influenced contemporary architects.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":316.76,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"Frank Newby":{"slug":"frank-newby","body":"Frank Newby (26 March 1926 – 10 May 2001) was one of the leading structural engineers of the 20th century, working with such architects as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Powell_(architect)\">Philip Powell</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hidalgo_Moya\">Hidalgo Moya</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eero_Saarinen\">Eero Saarinen</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Price\">Cedric Price</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Stirling_(architect)\">James Stirling</a>, and the practice of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skidmore,_Owings_and_Merrill\">Skidmore, Owings and Merrill</a> (SOM), and such engineers as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ove_Arup\">Ove Arup</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_Samuely\">Felix Samuely</a>.","subtitle":"Structural engineer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/frank-newby.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Newby","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=London,+UK","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1189.84,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":350,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":449.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":552.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1239.4,"topicId":"food-and-drink","topicLabel":"Food and drink"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":833.88,"topicId":"hot-stuff-club","topicLabel":"Hot Stuff Club"}]},"FUN PALACE":{"slug":"fun-palace","body":"Fun Palace is Cedric Price’s most celebrated work. Whether characterised as a giant toy or as a building-sized transformable machine, the project’s interest resides in its radical reliance on structure and technology, its exemplification of notions of time-based and anticipatory architecture. With Fun Palace, Price addressed social and political issues that go far beyond the typical bounds of architecture. Fun Palace was conceived and commissioned in 1961 by renowned theatre director and producer Joan Littlewood. Price developed plans for the project through 1964, both for the main project and for a smaller, more mobile “pilot” project. Neither were realised. Attempts to get planning permission for a wide variety of sites within and around London continued through 1970, amidst opposition from church, citizen groups and confounded city councils.","subtitle":"Laboratory of Fun and a University of the Streets","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/fun-palace.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/283-cedric-price-fun-palace","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.518722,-0.0007261,13z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":139.88,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":464.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":647.24,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":11,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":381,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":671.76,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1034.84,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1547.08,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":414.28,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1021.72,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1036.76,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1132.72,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":873.92,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1684.84,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":744.8,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":796.08,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":883.28,"topicId":"influence","topicLabel":"Influence"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":676.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1117.36,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1903.76,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":651.64,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":83.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":138.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":637.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":681.84,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1943.6,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1104.36,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"}]},"GLC":{"slug":"glc","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">The Greater London Council (GLC) was the top-tier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_government\">local government</a> administrative body for <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greater_London\">Greater London</a> from 1965 to 1986. It replaced the earlier <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_County_Council\">London County Council</a> (LCC) which had covered a much smaller area. The GLC was dissolved in 1986 by the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_Government_Act_1985\">Local Government Act 1985</a> and its powers were devolved to the London boroughs and other entities.</p>","subtitle":"Greater London Council","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/glc.jpg","external":"http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/march/31/newsid_2530000/2530803.stm","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":205.2,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":492.2,"topicId":"southbank","topicLabel":"SOUTHBANK"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":143,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1549.2,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"InterAction Centre":{"slug":"interaction-centre","body":"The failure of the Fun Palace was not the end of Price's attempts to realize an interactive and improvisational architecture. In 1976, he built a greatly reduced version of the Fun Palace in Kentish Town. Known as the InterAction Centre, this design incorporated many of the features and innovations of the Fun Palace, though on a smaller scale. It was demolished in 2003.","subtitle":"Kentish Town","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/interaction-centre.jpg","external":"http://www.audacity.org/SM-26-11-07-02.htm","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Kentish+Town/@51.55031,-0.14071,14z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":500.68,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":411.76,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":580.12,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1022.52,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":632.24,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1300.44,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":15.88,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"}]},"Joan Littlewood":{"slug":"joan-littlewood","body":"Joan Maud Littlewood (6 October 1914 – 20 September 2002) was an English theatre director, best known for her work in developing the left-wing <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Workshop\">Theatre Workshop</a>. She has been called \"The Mother of Modern Theatre\". Littlewood and her company lived and slept in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theatre_Royal_Stratford_East\">Theatre Royal</a> while it was restored. Productions of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Alchemist_(play)\">The Alchemist</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_II_(play)\">Richard II</a>, the latter starring <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harry_H._Corbett\">Harry H. Corbett</a> in the title role, established the reputation of the company. She also conceived and developed the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Fun_Palace&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\">Fun Palace</a> in collaboration with architect <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Price\">Cedric Price</a>, an experimental model of a participatory social environment that, although never realized, has become an important influence in the architecture of the 20th and 21st centuries.","subtitle":"Theatre Director","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/joan-littlewood.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_Littlewood","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/United+Kingdom/@55.3617609,-3.4433238,5z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":395.96,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1096.72,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":418.72,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1044.16,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":928.16,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":408.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":32.56,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":727.36,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1102.68,"topicId":"fun-palace","topicLabel":"FUN PALACE"}]},"La Villette Greenhouse":{"slug":"la-villette-greenhouse","body":"SERRE is a proposal for a greenhouse located in the Parc de la Villette in Paris, north of the canal de l’Ourq and adjacent to the lock on the canal de st. Denis. (Opera, p. 10)\r\n\r\nPaul Finch: There was an intriguing moment where it looked like Cedric Price might do some sort of pavilion at La Villette. It was designed with Frank Newby engineers – quite a substantial job – but I got the impression that the budgets were starting to be trimmed and it was never realised.\r\n\r\nBernard Tschumi: This is exactly what happened. Being the chief architect of the whole operation, I had asked a few people to design something. In the case of Cedric it was a pretty substantial greenhouse and he had worked quite seriously on it, but it had to be financed by both public and private money, and as the private money never came, it was never done. You might be amused to know that in the new Paris Zoo I just completed, the greenhouse is a sort of silent homage to that very one designed by Cedric.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":542.6,"topicId":"vilette","topicLabel":"VILETTE"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":207.6,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":526.28,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":421.56,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"London Zoo Aviary":{"slug":"london-zoo-aviary","body":"The Aviary at London Zoo is one of the few built projects, realised in collaboration with the engineer Frank Newby and Lord Snowdon. This aviary has been a landmark on the London skyline since it was built in 1962. It was the first walk-through aviary in existence - pushing the architectural boundaries of its time. This aviary is home to some of the most majestic birds in the world, including white ibis and peacocks.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1176.4,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":404.24,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1366.32,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":662.72,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":713.16,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1719.8,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":982.08,"topicId":"architecture","topicLabel":"Architecture"}]},"McAlpine (company)":{"slug":"mcalpine-company","body":"Sir Robert McAlpine is a private British company headquartered in Hertfordshire. It carries out engineering and construction for the oil and gas, petrochemical, power generation, nuclear, pharmaceutical, defence, chemical, water and mining industries.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":235.36,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":412.8,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":886.44,"topicId":"mcappy","topicLabel":"McAPPY"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":602.56,"topicId":"mcappy","topicLabel":"McAPPY"}]},"Piranesi":{"slug":"piranesi","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Piranesi was born in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mogliano_Veneto\">Mogliano Veneto</a>, near <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treviso\">Treviso</a>, then part of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Venice\">Republic of Venice</a>. His brother Andrea introduced him to <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin_language\">Latin</a> and the ancient civilization, and later he studied as an architect under his uncle, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matteo_Lucchesi\">Matteo Lucchesi</a>, who was Magistrato delle Acque, a Venetian engineer who specialized in excavation.</p>","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/piranesi.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Battista_Piranesi","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Rome,+Italy/@41.8723889,12.4801802,7z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":312,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"Pritzker Prize":{"slug":"pritzker-prize","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">The Pritzker Architecture Prize is awarded annually \"To honor a living <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> or architects whose built work demonstrates a combination of those qualities of talent, vision and commitment, which has produced consistent and significant contributions to humanity and the built environment through the art of architecture.\"</p>","subtitle":"Award","image":null,"external":"http://www.pritzkerprize.com","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/United+States/@37.09024,-95.712891,7z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":372.64,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"South Bank":{"slug":"south-bank","body":"In 1984 Price proposed the redevelopment of London's <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Bank\">South Bank</a>, and foresaw the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Eye\">London Eye</a> by suggesting that a giant <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferris_wheel\">Ferris wheel</a> should be constructed by the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_Thames\">River Thames</a>.\r\n\r\n“A city should be sufficiently old, rich and well-serviced to be able to accommodate a vast range of disparate, peculiar, incomparable objects, space, activities and people. The resultant mix, to be both convivial and convenient, is achieved through constant change in community appetites and individual mores – and not through a Band-Aid of architectural ingenuity trying to be all things to all men…”\r\n\r\n(Price Probes, BD, 16 September 1994)<!--more-->","subtitle":"Job no. 159","image":null,"external":"http://www.aaschool.ac.uk/PUBLIC/AAPUBLICATIONS/aboutpublications\n.php","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=london+eye","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":20.56,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":837.4,"topicId":"enabling","topicLabel":"Enabling"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":203.56,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":485,"topicId":"southbank","topicLabel":"SOUTHBANK"}]},"Steve Mullin":{"slug":"steve-mullin","body":"Stephen Mullin is an architect and housing consultant who worked for Cedric Price from 1964 to 1970 on numerous projects, including the Fun Palace and Potteries Thinkbelt; later taught at the Bartlett and the Architectural Association schools of architecture, and then formulated the Estate Action Programme for Islington Council in the mid-1970s. He is a former CABE design review panelist; organised the Housing Design Awards from 1993 to 2004, and is a co-author of ‘Non-Traditional Houses’, a distillation of the research programme for which he was responsible during his 16 years with the Housing Directorate of the Department of the Environment and its successors.","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/steve-mullin.jpg","external":"http://www.designforhomes.org/people/stephen-mullin/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/10+downing+street/@51.503364,-0.127625,15z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1123.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":510,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]},"Trade Union Congress":{"slug":"trade-union-congress","body":"The Trades Union Congress (TUC) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_trade_union_center\">national trade union centre</a>, a federation of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_union\">trade unions</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/England_and_Wales\">England and Wales</a>, representing the majority of trade unions. There are fifty-eight affiliated unions with a total of about 6.2 million members, around half of whom are represented by <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unite_the_Union\">Unite</a> or <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNISON\">UNISON</a>.","subtitle":"Trade Union Centre","image":null,"external":"http://www.tuc.org.uk","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=Uk","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":363,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1174.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":646.56,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":841.44,"topicId":"hot-stuff-club","topicLabel":"Hot Stuff Club"}]},"Yona Friedman":{"slug":"yona-friedman","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Yona Friedman (born 5 June 1923,<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budapest\">Budapest</a>) is a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hungary\">Hungarian</a>-born <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_people\">French</a> architect, urban planner and designer. He became famous in the late 1950s and early 1960s, in the so-called age of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megastructures_(architecture)\">megastructures</a>.</p>","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/yona-friedman.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yona_Friedman","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Paris,+France/@48.856614,2.3522219,7z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":345.68,"topicId":"building","topicLabel":"Building"}]}}}